/**
 * The driver's own UI: one host element on <html>, marked `data-reins="ignore"`, with a
 * closed shadow root holding the cursor, the control indicator, consent prompts, toasts
 * and highlights.
 *
 * Built for a strict CSP (`style-src 'self'`): styles come from a constructable
 * stylesheet, and positions are set through the CSSOM (`style.setProperty`), never
 * through a `style` attribute. Every dynamic string is inserted with `textContent`, and
 * every string an agent or the hub wrote is passed through `visible` first.
 *
 * What the page's own stylesheet can do to it is kept small. The stylesheet reads no custom
 * property and names no font family a page could define (`sealCss`), the host pins the
 * properties `all` leaves alone, and where the browser has the Popover API the host is shown
 * in the top layer, last, where no page content paints over it. `check()` says whether the
 * overlay can be relied on right now; while it cannot, no control that lets an agent do more
 * takes a click.
 *
 * Controls act on the person's input only: a real event, natively trusted, being dispatched
 * to the control itself (`primordials.ts`). An agent has no such event to give, and a script
 * in the page cannot forge one or hand a borrowed one to the overlay's listeners. That is the
 * whole of it: script that runs in the page's own JavaScript realm can defeat the overlay in
 * other ways, whichever loaded first, and is outside what this defends (docs/SECURITY.md).
 *
 * Keys are the person's input too, and often not meant for the overlay: a prompt takes the
 * focus while they type. No key answers a card unless the person plainly means the card
 * (`#next`). And no wait in here relies on a timer firing: every one is a time, compared with
 * the clock when a click, a key or a request arrives (`#sweep`).
 * The panel the indicator opens is in `panel.ts`.
 */
